# ONE LINE ENDING, EVERYWHERE. (DREAMCRM-99, deliverable 4.)
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG. This repository has always stored LF — measured, not
# assumed: on 2026-09-22 `git ls-files --eol` reported 2,577 tracked files at
# `i/lf`, 150 binary, 58 with no line ending at all, and ZERO at `i/crlf`. The
# committed bytes were never the problem.
#
# The WORKING TREE was. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its system
# config, so a Windows checkout of this repo materialises every one of those
# 2,577 files with CRLF — 276,016 CRLF lines against 1,342 bare LF on the tree
# this file was written in — while every Linux checkout, CI's included, gets LF.
# Nothing in the repository said otherwise, because there was no `.gitattributes`
# at all.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERS HERE MORE THAN IN MOST REPOS. A large share of this repo's
# test suite is guards that READ FILES OFF DISK and grade what they find —
# `quotedChunks`, the control-byte rule, the legibility floor, the tree-wide
# sweeps in `tests/guards/**`. Every one of them sees different bytes on a Windows
# author's machine than on the runner that gates the merge. A `\r` is a non-word
# character, it terminates a line early, it sits inside a captured group, and it
# ends up in a snapshot. On #657 two independently built tree-wide audits —
# Vesper's assertion and Sentinel's — shared one blind spot for exactly this
# reason. That is not a coincidence between two people; it is the environment
# both were standing in.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CHANGES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It changes the working tree, not
# the history: `git add --renormalize .` against this file produces an EMPTY
# diff, because the index was already LF. There is no renormalization commit to
# time against in-flight PRs, and no merge in the queue can conflict with one.
# What it does is make a fresh checkout LF on every platform, so a guard reads
# the same bytes wherever it runs.
#
# AN EXISTING WINDOWS TREE DOES NOT REFRESH ITSELF. Checking this file out leaves
# already-materialised files as they are — `git status` stays clean, because
# `text` normalises on check-IN and a CRLF file still hashes to the LF blob. To
# take the new endings in a tree you already have:
#
#     git add --renormalize .      # confirms: nothing to commit
#     git rm --cached -r . -q && git reset --hard
#
# `tests/guards/line-endings.test.ts` grades the committed bytes, which is the
# half that is true on every platform. It deliberately does NOT grade the working
# tree: that would fail an otherwise-correct stale Windows checkout and teach
# people to ignore it.

# Text is detected by content, stored as LF, and CHECKED OUT as LF everywhere.
# `eol=lf` is the half `text=auto` alone does not give you — without it the
# checkout still honours `core.autocrlf`, which is the whole defect above.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, named rather than left to detection. `text=auto` gets these right
# today; naming them means a new .jpg cannot be misdetected as text and have a
# 0x0d inside it rewritten. Derived from what is actually tracked — jpg (114),
# png (15), webp (14), woff2 (6), ico (1) — plus the neighbouring formats this
# repo would plausibly add next.
*.avif  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.webm  binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
